  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different chronic lung diseases create local airway environments that favor distinct bacterial communities. A central lung diagram is linked to disease-specific close-ups showing structural damage, inflammation, and altered chemistry.\n\nIn emphysema, normal alveoli are contrasted with fewer, enlarged alveoli. This reduces gas-exchange surface area and lowers oxygen levels, which appears to promote anaerobic bacteria. In chronic bronchitis, inflamed airways with neutrophils are associated with a drop in pH. The panel suggests that this acidic environment supports acid-tolerant bacteria such as Lactobacilli, while other groups decline.\n\nTwo cystic fibrosis views emphasize mucus accumulation and inflammation. One shows a thick mucus layer lining the airway, where bacteria are present within mucus-rich regions; this seems to create a niche for P. aeruginosa. The other highlights epithelial cells, neutrophilic inflammation, and metabolic changes. These changes increase the availability of alanine and lactate, which are shown feeding the growth of P. aeruginosa.\n\nOverall, the message is that lung disease does not simply add infection; it reshapes oxygen, pH, mucus, and nutrient conditions, and these altered microenvironments select for different microbes in different respiratory disorders.",
